      "q": "5. Find two numbers such that their sum is\u00a0 $ 6 $\u00a0 and the product is\u00a0 $ 14. $\nAns.\nLet $x$ and $y$ be the no.\nGiven that, $x + y = 6$\n$xy = 14$\n${x^2} - 6x + 14 = 0$\n$D =\u00a0 - 20$\nWe have , $x = \\dfrac{{ - b \\pm \\sqrt D }}{{2a}}$\nSubstitute the values,\n$x = \\dfrac{{ - ( - 6) \\pm \\sqrt { - 20} }}{{2 \\times 1}}$\n$ = \\dfrac{{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt 5 i}}{2}$\n$ = 3 \\pm \\sqrt 5 {\\text{i}}$\nWhen $x = 3 + \\sqrt 5 {\\text{i}}$\n$y = 6 - (3 + \\sqrt 5 {\\text{i}})$\n$ = 3 - \\sqrt 5 {\\text{i}}$\nWhen $x = 3 - \\sqrt 5 {\\text{i}}$\n$y = 6 - (3 - \\sqrt 5 {\\text{i}})$\n$ = 3 + \\sqrt 5 {\\text{i}}$\nTherefore, the numbers are $3 + \\sqrt 5 {\\text{i}}$ and $3 - \\sqrt 5 {\\text{i}}$.",
